course: dictionary
lesson: 1391
title: Learning a word from its sentences
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A capuchin is a word you may meet in a science book or a history book. Read the words around it and they will tell you what it means. «Wild capuchin monkeys in Brazil crack hard nuts with stones on a flat rock.» The clues are monkeys, nuts and stones, so a capuchin must be an animal.
explain: The sentences give more clues. «Squirrel monkeys travel in big troops and often follow capuchin monkeys to find fruit trees.» Here capuchin monkeys are animals that lead the way to food, so a capuchin is a kind of monkey.
explain: Some words have two meanings. Capuchin can also mean a Capuchin friar, a monk who wears a brown robe with a pointed hood. The clue is the topic: church and history lessons are about friars, and science lessons are about monkeys. Always swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A capuchin is a small, clever monkey from Central and South America.
fact: Capuchin monkeys have a cap of dark fur on their heads.
fact: Capuchin monkeys use tools, such as stones, to crack nuts.
fact: A Capuchin friar is a member of a Catholic order of monks who wear brown robes with pointed hoods.
fact: «Capuchin» has two meanings, so look at the clues around it to find the right one.
exercise: «Wild capuchin monkeys in Brazil crack hard nuts with stones.» What do capuchins use stones for? | To build houses | *To crack nuts | To sell at the market | To throw into rivers
exercise: In church and history topics, what colour are the robes of a Capuchin friar? | Green | Blue | White | *Brown
exercise: «Squirrel monkeys often follow capuchin monkeys to find fruit trees.» Find and copy one word that tells you capuchins are animals. | follow | fruit | *monkeys | trees
test: Some stone tools used by capuchins are about 3,000 years old. What does this tell you? | Capuchins are a kind of stone | *Capuchins have used stones to crack nuts for a very long time | Capuchins live in castles | Capuchins are a new kind of tool
test: The word capuchin can mean a monkey or a friar. What clue shows which one is meant? | The number of letters in the word | *The words around it, such as nuts and stones, or friars and robes | The colour of the page | Nothing, so you must guess
exam: Mia reads «Capuchins are clever» and thinks it must mean a coffee drink. What is the best check? | Look only at the first letter | Ask someone and never check the sentence | Skip the word and never come back to it | *Swap in a meaning: a clever monkey or friar fits, but a coffee drink does not
